### Is @FunctionalInterface mandatory?

No. Any interface with exactly one abstract method is a functional interface and can be the target of a lambda expression, regardless of whether the annotation is present.

### Why is the annotation recommended?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// WITHOUT annotation: works, but no safety net
interface AccountFilter {
    boolean test(String accountId, BigDecimal balance);
}
// AccountFilter f = (id, bal) -> bal.signum() > 0; // compiles fine

// Later, someone adds a second method:
// interface AccountFilter {
//     boolean test(String accountId, BigDecimal balance);
//     boolean testStrict(String accountId, BigDecimal balance); // 2 abstract!
// }
// Every lambda using AccountFilter now fails, but the error points HERE, not at the interface.

// WITH annotation: error caught at the SOURCE
@FunctionalInterface
interface SafeAccountFilter {
    boolean test(String accountId, BigDecimal balance);
    // boolean testStrict(String accountId, BigDecimal balance);
    // COMPILE ERROR RIGHT HERE: "Multiple non-overriding abstract methods"
}
```

### Can a functional interface extend another interface?

Yes, as long as the total abstract method count remains exactly one.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Parent with 1 abstract method:
interface Describable { String describe(); }

// WRONG: 2 abstract methods (execute + describe)
// @FunctionalInterface
// interface AccountAction extends Describable {
//     void execute(); // COMPILE ERROR with @FunctionalInterface
// }

// CORRECT: override parent's abstract as default
@FunctionalInterface
interface AccountAction extends Describable {
    void execute(); // 1 abstract method
    @Override
    default String describe() { return "Account action"; }
}

// Extending a marker interface (0 abstract methods):
@FunctionalInterface
interface SerializableAction extends java.io.Serializable {
    void execute(); // 1 abstract (0 from parent + 1 from this)
}
```

### How do default and static methods work with lambdas?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;

@FunctionalInterface
interface FeeCalculator {
    BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal tradeAmount); // THE abstract method

    // Default: composition
    default FeeCalculator withMinimum(BigDecimal min) {
        return amount -> {
            BigDecimal fee = this.calculate(amount);
            return fee.compareTo(min) < 0 ? min : fee;
        };
    }

    default FeeCalculator withMaximum(BigDecimal max) {
        return amount -> {
            BigDecimal fee = this.calculate(amount);
            return fee.compareTo(max) > 0 ? max : fee;
        };
    }

    // Static: factory
    static FeeCalculator percentageFee(BigDecimal rate) {
        return amount -> amount.multiply(rate).setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }

    static FeeCalculator flatFee(BigDecimal fee) {
        return amount -> fee;
    }
}

public class FeeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FeeCalculator brokerage = FeeCalculator.percentageFee(new BigDecimal("0.001"))
            .withMinimum(new BigDecimal("20.00"))
            .withMaximum(new BigDecimal("2500.00"));

        BigDecimal fee = brokerage.calculate(new BigDecimal("500000"));
        System.out.println("Brokerage: Rs." + fee);
    }
}
```

```text
Brokerage: Rs.500.00
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.core;

// WRONG: forgetting @FunctionalInterface and adding a second method later
interface TransferValidator {
    boolean validate(String from, String to);
    // Someone adds this months later:
    // boolean validateAmount(java.math.BigDecimal amount);
    // Every lambda breaks with confusing errors at usage sites
}

// CORRECT: always annotate
@FunctionalInterface
interface SafeTransferValidator {
    boolean validate(String from, String to);
}
```

### How is this different from a regular interface?

A regular interface can have any number of abstract methods. A functional interface is constrained to exactly one. This constraint is what makes lambda expressions possible: the compiler knows which method the lambda implements.

### What about the standard library examples?

Runnable, Comparable, Comparator, Callable, and all java.util.function interfaces are annotated with @FunctionalInterface. UnaryOperator extends Function<T,T> and inherits apply as its single abstract method. BinaryOperator extends BiFunction<T,T,T> and inherits apply as its single abstract method.

### What should a senior engineer know?

The @FunctionalInterface annotation is a documentation and safety tool. It communicates design intent to other developers: this interface is designed for lambdas. It catches design mistakes at the source. It costs nothing at runtime. Omitting it is like omitting @Override: technically valid but a missed opportunity for compile-time safety.
